[implementation/language] Name implementation language (reviewing)
Prototype implementation language: Rust 2024 using Cargo and rustc 1.94. The executable crate is `crates/grimoire`; library modules remain small and dependency-light. Mechanical work proceeds through focused unit and integration fixtures, with a commit after each validated slice.
Accept: Cargo check/test runs from the repository root and every implementation task names its owned module or fixture paths.
> 2026-08-26: Prototype implementation language is Rust 2024, chosen to match the neighboring Scry repository and the available rustc/cargo 1.94 toolchain; reversible before public API freeze.
> 2026-08-26: The initial executable crate is `crates/grimoire`, a library-first crate with a thin binary only if an end-to-end CLI check later proves useful.
---
[schemas/meta/scalar-domain] Resolve finite scalar refinement (reviewing)
Prototype ruling: resolve the undefined finite-scalar refinement as the closed boolean scalar `true | false`. Keep positive-integer, finite-number, text, and closed enumeration as separate refinements. Add a fixture that can falsify this choice if the full case set requires another primitive.
Accept: Bootstrap schema validation has one executable scalar contract; boolean values round-trip; non-boolean values fail the finite-scalar fixture visibly.
-> schemas/meta
> 2026-08-26: For the first executable prototype, finite-scalar means boolean with exactly the values true and false; this is a provisional ruling subject to fixture pressure, not a frozen vocabulary amendment.
---
[grammar/namespace-uri] Resolve namespace URI grammar (reviewing)
Prototype ruling: namespace identifiers are exact quoted ASCII strings whose payload begins with `https://`, has a nonempty authority, and contains no whitespace or control characters. Percent-encoding, host case, path spelling, and escapes remain exact bytes; no normalization or dereference occurs.
Accept: Offline parser validation accepts the provisional form, rejects relative/http/whitespace/control forms, and preserves exact spelling through serialization.
-> extension-namespaces/minting
> 2026-08-26: The prototype namespace URI rule is an exact ASCII https URI string with nonempty authority and no whitespace/control characters; percent-encoding and case are never normalized.
---
[grammar/unknown-extension] Resolve opaque extension boundary (reviewing)
Prototype ruling: an unrecognized extension parameter is preserved as the raw source span beginning at `extension` and ending at its terminating semicolon, including internal comments and insignificant whitespace. Known parameters use canonical serialization.
Accept: A mixed known/unknown fixture proves raw unknown bytes survive parse/serialize while known values are canonicalized.
-> grammar/namespace-uri
> 2026-08-26: The prototype opaque boundary is the complete unknown extension-parameter source span from the `extension` keyword through its terminating semicolon, inclusive; this is an explicit canonical-serialization exception.
---
[architecture/indexed-visibility] Resolve indexed visibility structure (reviewing)
Prototype ruling: indexed modality and time visibility expands to ordinary addressed elements and directed connections before validation. No compact indexed grammar is introduced in v1. A small fixture proves the expanded connection set for two time steps and two modalities.
Accept: Block-causal and mixed-attention fixtures distinguish structural visibility from finalized attention metadata and report generated addresses deterministically.
-> architecture/vocabulary
-> projlang/design
> 2026-08-26: The first implementation expands indexed visibility into ordinary addressed connections before structural validation; compact indexed syntax is deferred until the expanded fixture exposes real duplication or size pressure.
---
[architecture/parameter-lineage] Resolve parameter-state lineage (reviewing)
Prototype ruling: parameter states, deltas, continual updates, and merges remain external lineage artifacts or schema-governed values in v1. They are never activation connections and do not become new frozen element kinds. A reproducibility fixture records a DARE/TIES-style merge as visibly deferred until a future artifact relation exists.
Accept: The fixture preserves logical architecture identity, names base/delta/result provenance, and returns a distinct deferred outcome rather than misrepresenting the merge as graph flow.
-> architecture/vocabulary
-> schemas/inventory
> 2026-08-26: V1 keeps parameter-state lineage outside the core graph as an external artifact or finalized candidate value; no checkpoint, delta, or merge vocabulary is added to the frozen core by the prototype.
> 2026-08-26: A parameter merge is not represented as an activation connection; reproducibility is either supplied by an external lineage artifact or reported as deferred.
